   244	## 3. Degree bookkeeping on the compactified map
   245	
   246	**Lemma 3.1 (conservation of number).**  Let `z in P^2` with `Phi^{-1}(z)`
   247	finite.  Then
   248	
   249	```text
   250	sum_{p in Phi^{-1}(z)} mult_p(Phi) = N,                            (3.1)
   251	```
   252	
   253	where `mult_p(Phi) = dim_C O_{X,p} / Phi^# m_{P^2,z} O_{X,p}` is the local
   254	degree (equivalently the topological local mapping degree at the isolated
   255	preimage `p`).
   256	
   257	*Proof.*  The set `Delta subset P^2` of points with positive-dimensional
   258	`Phi`-fibre is finite (image of the exceptional curve `Exc`), and `z notin
   259	Delta` by hypothesis.  Choose an open ball `W ni z` with
   260	`W intersect Delta = empty`.  Then `Phi : Phi^{-1}(W) -> W` is proper (as
   261	`X` is compact) with all fibres finite, hence finite; `W` is connected and
   262	`Phi` is surjective, so this finite map has a well-defined degree, equal to
   263	the number of preimages of a point of `W` over which it is unramified.  The
   264	locus in `P^2` with fewer than `N` preimages is a proper closed subvariety,
   265	so a general point of `W` has exactly `N` preimages, all lying in
   266	`Phi^{-1}(W)`.  Hence the degree is `N`, and conservation of number for a
   267	finite flat-over-a-smooth-base map (equivalently, additivity of the
   268	topological degree over an isolated fibre) gives (3.1).  QED
   269	
   270	**Corollary 3.2 (raw fibre identity).**  For `z in C^2` with `Phi^{-1}(z)`
   271	finite,
   272	
   273	```text
   274	N = sum_{p in F^{-1}(z)} mult_p(F)  +  sum_{p in Phi^{-1}(z) cap D} mult_p(Phi).
   275	                                                                   (3.2)
   276	```
   277	If moreover `F` is etale (e.g. Keller), every finite preimage has
   278	`mult_p(F)=1` and the first sum is the *set* count `f(z)`:
   279	
