+For K1–K3 use `t=xy,w=xy^2` where indicated; solving the monomials gives exactly the substitutions in Opus 657–706. For K4, `(w^2+w)/t` becomes `-xi+t xi^2`, and the bounded solution at `x=0` is `y=b`, so `2=1+1` (Opus 708–730). The Jacobians are respectively `x`, `2xy`, `4x^2y^3`, and `2xy+1`; none is a Keller map. The controls correctly test the multiplicity bookkeeping, and K3 genuinely displays why `s_l` belongs in the general formula. Only the inference from K3 to the ledger's Keller-scoped (2.3) fails, as detailed in Section 3.
